From 575d1121fbd843d8f9bd795d7eab2dc2573ad442 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Romain Dolbeau <romain@dolbeau.org>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 11:19:07 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 7/9] hw/scsi/sun3-si, hw/m68k/sun3: choose between the two
 chips

The board grows core=auto|sw|rtl and the machine grows si-core= to
reach it.  Everything that makes this board a Sun-3 board rather than a
5380 - the Am9516 chain parsing, the packing FIFO, the CSR semantics,
the "the OBIO si ignores fifo_count during a DATA phase" rule, the VME
register shadows, and the three status-bit decisions SunOS settled - is
untouched.  Eight one-line dispatchers are the whole difference, because
all of that logic is about the chip's observable pins and not about how
the chip is built.

Two things did have to change, and both are about who wakes the board
up.

The pump no longer spins.  It used to sit in `while (!drq) run_ns(100)`
because a Verilated core only advances while it is being stepped, so
stepping it was the only way to see DRQ come up.  With the software chip
that loop would be worse than pointless: scsi_req_enqueue on a real disk
completes on QEMU's AIO path, which cannot run while this function holds
the thread inside an MMIO handler, so the transfer it is waiting for
could never finish.  It returns instead, and the chip's DRQ pin brings
it back.  This is the same shape as esp.c's dma_cb, which lets a command
defer itself until an external DMA controller says go.

And the chip's interrupt is now listened to rather than polled.  On the
RTL path the board notices by reading si_irq() at the foot of every
register access and from a 500 us timer; the software chip has no timer
and needs none, but an interrupt raised between two accesses - which is
every interrupt that ends a transfer - would otherwise sit in the chip
until the driver happened to touch a register, and a driver waiting for
that interrupt never will.  Connecting the pin cost a third of the
"lost interrupt" reports on a SunOS boot.

The interrupt callback pumps before it updates, because that is also how
a *short* transfer ends: when the target gives less than was asked for,
the chip stops asking and never raises DRQ again, so DRQ cannot be what
notices.  It interrupts on the phase mismatch instead, which is exactly
what the pump's end-of-transfer test is waiting for.

The machine's block_default_type becomes IF_SCSI, so a bare -drive
file=x is a SCSI disk here as it is on every other machine whose only
disk interface is SCSI.  It reaches nothing unless the board was asked
for.

Verified: the 3/60 PROM reads a Sun label and loads an 893 KB kernel;
NetBSD 10.1 attaches si0, finds the disk, mounts root and reaches a
shell over 600 commands and 569 DMA transfers with residual 0 on every
one; SunOS 4.1.1 boots multi-user through fsck, rc and the network
services.  The RTL path is unchanged and still boots NetBSD.
